window.docContents[103].resolve({"/Useless-Ramblings/The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-useless-language-based-problem-framing/#The-Den--Useless-Ramblings--The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-useless-language-based-problem-framing":{"contents":"Fair warning -- this is a terribly confusing post to read, don't read it unless you want to be confused by my terrible explanations. I'll probably try and add diagrams and more formalization to help, but I just wanted to get this thought out of my head first.\n\nMotivation\n\nI was looking for a way to generically describe the headaches that come with working with hardware, but which are absent with software, and found that you could frame basically any problem as some kind of leaky-abstraction-esque situation. It's not very useful, but I want to write it down since it's an old idea of mine.\n\nOnce, I was building an E-paper laptop, and burnt out a component by running current in the wrong direction. From there, in order to complete the E-paper laptop, I'd have to look online for vendors, pay some money, and wait for the new component to arrive in a month or so. Time and money -- it was such a drag! In contrast, in a software project you could bork your entire installation, wipe it clean and restart at the cost of a few hours and no money at all. From this experience, a friend and I coined the term the Curse of Hardware.\n\n(Yes, I know software can have similar problems too -- this is just the experience I had.)\n\nWhat is the Curse of Hardware?\n\nSimply put, the Curse of Hardware is when you want to write a program in some language that does not include certain undesirable features -- however, the compile target that your language must compile into is forcing your language to include those features.\n\nIn the previous example, after I'd burnt out my component:\n\n* I wanted to complete the E-paper laptop (the \"program\" I wanted to write)* without spending time and money the way I ended up doing (I wanted to write the complete_E_paper_laptop program using a language that has no concept of time or money)* but of course, the universe does not work this way (the compile target refuses to cooperate)\n\nWhy is there a higher-level language and a compile target involved in the situation? The higher-level language is just whatever human-readable concept I come up in my mind to describe the \"program\"; i.e. \"look for vendors that sell this component\", \"ask the school for funding\", etc. As for the compile target, since we're talking about actions within the real-life universe (like building a laptop or talking to vendors), I'm imagining that there is some arbitrarily low-level description of the universe (say, an atom-by-atom description, and you can probably go lower -- I ain't a physicist) that your human-readable actions eventually translate to when you take them.\n\nSo at the end of the day, this is basically a convoluted way of shaking your fist at the universe when it doesn't let you do things your way.\n\nWhat is this useful for?\n\nUh, nothing probably.\n\nI do like it because it helps frame basically any problem in terms of languages, and I like languages. Unsurprisingly, it tends to be more useful when the \"compile target\" is a bit higher-level than the arbitrarily low-level machine code analogue of the universe.\n\nFor instance, let's say:\n\n* I want to want to go on a trip that involves both hiking and swimming pools (the \"program\" I want to write)* I only want to pack one kind of shoe; hiking boots or slippers (I wanted to write the go_on_a_trip program using a language where I am restricted to only packing one kind of shoe)* but of course, the universe does not work this way. Instead of going directly to the arbitrarily low \"universe assembly\", it suffices to think of a lower-level language describing the universe that includes the facts that hiking needs hiking boots, and swimming pools require slippers.\n\n","context":"The Den\u0009Useless Ramblings","header":"3.1. The Curse of Hardware -- A useless language-based problem framing","id":"/Useless-Ramblings/The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-useless-language-based-problem-framing/#The-Den--Useless-Ramblings--The-Curse-of-Hardware----A-useless-language-based-problem-framing"}});